<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>Deutsche Welle: DW.com - Sports</title><subtitle type="html">Deutsche Welle: DW.com - Sports</subtitle><id>https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-sports</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:47Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss-en-sports" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.dw.com/s-8171?maca=en-rss-en-sports-1027-xml-mrss" /><link rel="via" href="https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-sports" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>Formula 1: Rwanda to bring sport back to Africa</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:bb450a85bf6465577ad00a412c52af93855ad4004075b91a3420b01524e95447</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:47Z</updated><published>2026-10-13T11:20:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/formula-1-rwanda-to-bring-sport-back-to-africa/a-79649217--ee284b399c6d.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/formula-1-rwanda-to-bring-sport-back-to-africa/a-79649217?maca=en-rss-en-sports-1027-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">The last time Formula 1 hosted a race in Africa was way back in 1993. But a new track in Rwanda is set to host a Grand Prix in "the early 2030s," continuing the country’s investment in international sport.</summary><category term="Sports" /></entry><entry><title>Will Formula 1 return to South Africa?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:e1b83593cae9598f30a4e66dfb35d97aa66a27e12c3905454e30fa36fe50be93</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:47Z</updated><published>2024-09-26T12:12:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/will-formula-1-return-to-south-africa/a-70269906--f86898c05871.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/will-formula-1-return-to-south-africa/a-70269906?maca=en-rss-en-sports-1027-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">The last F1 race in Africa was in 1993. A return could be in the cards, but what would it mean for the country and the continent?</summary><category term="Sports" /></entry><entry><title>World Cup 2034: Does Saudi Arabia shelving Neom Stadium spell trouble?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:9fb167618da8f90391088dfb2ee3fc5d1ced141b663db7dacf6f201d697a7aa3</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:47Z</updated><published>2026-10-09T11:43:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/world-cup-2034-does-saudi-arabia-shelving-neom-stadium-spell-trouble/a-79592948--b8b608a266b9.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/world-cup-2034-does-saudi-arabia-shelving-neom-stadium-spell-trouble/a-79592948?maca=en-rss-en-sports-1027-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Since winning the right to hold the 2034 World Cup unopposed, Saudi Arabia has been pulling away from sport. With reports that its most ambitious stadium project will be abandoned, what does this mean for the World Cup?</summary><category term="Sports" /></entry><entry><title>Ronaldo stays on under cloud as Messi exits internationals</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:d92e2688fcb2f0c3d88a0d0b6ff55040cdf7cef84a81b194e6c03e8238aa3085</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:47Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T09:23:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/ronaldo-stays-on-under-cloud-as-messi-exits-internationals/a-79574551--518c4a71824f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/ronaldo-stays-on-under-cloud-as-messi-exits-internationals/a-79574551?maca=en-rss-en-sports-1027-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Just hours before Lionel Messi played his last match for Argentina, Cristiano Ronaldo posted his latest salvo in a row with Portugal. Ronaldo wants to play on despite the troubles, while Messi bathed in adulation.</summary><category term="Sports" /></entry><entry><title>Football: Lionel Messi plays final game for Argentina</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:f9195d26f36a7ff48ccdcec79ef2f3e527615a43fe246b4cf44b9898a7436ce3</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:47Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T02:35:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/football-lionel-messi-plays-final-game-for-argentina/a-79570608--6687ff69f964.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/football-lionel-messi-plays-final-game-for-argentina/a-79570608?maca=en-rss-en-sports-1027-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Argentinian superstar Lionel Messi bowed out of international football with one last goal for his country. For football in Argentina, and indeed across the world, it's the end of an era.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>Munich up against at least six competitors for 2036 Olympics</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:d6bcdc6b886d1317616750365d3d3c241402c2cfe9df98acabc19ee6589f5a8f</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:47Z</updated><published>2026-10-06T19:54:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/munich-up-against-at-least-six-competitors-for-2036-olympics/a-79566678--5b4a69f8bd09.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/munich-up-against-at-least-six-competitors-for-2036-olympics/a-79566678?maca=en-rss-en-sports-1027-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">The International Olympic Committee has revealed the six other countries intending to bid for the 2036 Olympic Games. This comes after Germany selected Munich as its Olympic candidate.</summary><category term="Sports" /></entry><entry><title>Germany's Franz Reindl elected new IIHF president</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:19c194e0d963f0cfb5169e2635bb3a07c86652884a9ab6b52197a433b9dae9cf</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:47Z</updated><published>2026-10-02T14:29:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/germany-s-franz-reindl-elected-new-iihf-president/a-79521799--24f017ae08ee.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/germany-s-franz-reindl-elected-new-iihf-president/a-79521799?maca=en-rss-en-sports-1027-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Franz Reindl has been elected president of ice hockey's world governing body. The former forward was a member of West Germany's Olympic bronze medal-winning team.</summary><category term="Sports" /></entry><entry><title>Senegal's Youth Olympics makes Olympics in Africa 'logical'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:e85945a01edbb5bf6f48b065d15d2b7a20b77bbf2f371c2f3e4f80db76d5abba</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:47Z</updated><published>2026-09-30T15:12:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/senegal-s-youth-olympics-makes-olympics-in-africa-logical/a-79468975--7dbe2801c775.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/senegal-s-youth-olympics-makes-olympics-in-africa-logical/a-79468975?maca=en-rss-en-sports-1027-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">The Youth Olympics are to be hosted in Africa for the first time ever. The edition in Senegal is also a chance to prove the continent can host a full Olympics in the future.</summary><category term="Sports" /></entry><entry><title>Women's football in Germany booming, but still much to do</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:d2202928b8e2b1643db54555bc09c31b9e218f826467d01bd20df918d7a65e88</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:47Z</updated><published>2026-09-19T04:42:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/women-s-football-in-germany-booming-but-still-much-to-do/a-79291687--313365dcb46e.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/women-s-football-in-germany-booming-but-still-much-to-do/a-79291687?maca=en-rss-en-sports-1027-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Women's football is growing rapidly, particularly in Germany. Nevertheless, equal pay and investment remain major talking points.</summary><category term="Sports" /></entry><entry><title>Dual citizenship means Germany now in competition for talent</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:ee69ff97617679f7d9bc1d4e9d57b8b5847f41f8a92c86a564314185b73d22c8</id><updated>2026-10-14T05:04:47Z</updated><published>2026-09-18T14:04:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/dual-citizenship-means-germany-now-in-competition-for-talent/a-79330797--d43696518a30.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/dual-citizenship-means-germany-now-in-competition-for-talent/a-79330797?maca=en-rss-en-sports-1027-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Coach Jürgen Klopp has named several players to his first Germany squad who are eligible to play for other countries. Some still aren't committed to playing for thje national team long-term.</summary><category term="Sports" /></entry></feed>
